# Colour after the noun and blanco or blanca to match el or la: the order and the agreement are Spanish's, so the lesson is written for Spanish.
lesson language-the-black-cat v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=7 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[colour.the-black-cat|cap]]"
  goal "Put a colour with the thing it describes in [[language.name]], in the right order and with the right ending."

  language es {
    look {
      scene 35x14 {
        column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
          comic pets who=["girl", "boy", "gran"] says=["Tengo un gato negro.", "Tengo una gata blanca.", "Mi perro es marrón."] moods=["happy", "happy", "happy"] with=["cat", "cat", "dog"] thinks=[0, 0, 0] holds=["", "", ""] labels=["1", "2", "3"]
        }
      }
      say """
        In Spanish the colour comes after the thing: el gato negro is the black cat. When the word
        ends in a and takes la, the colour ends in a too: la casa blanca, the white house. A colour
        that ends in e, like verde, or in a consonant, like azul, stays the same: el mar azul, la
        hierba verde.
        """
    }
    do {
      level easy {
        worked language.es-the-black-cat-name k=4
      }
      show language.es-the-black-cat-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
      show language.es-the-black-cat-meaning k=3 easy-k=2
      show language.es-the-black-cat-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=4
      show language.es-the-black-cat-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
      show language.es-the-black-cat-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
      show language.es-the-black-cat-write k=5 easy-k=6 hard-k=3
      show language.es-the-black-cat-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
      show language.es-the-black-cat-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
      level hard {
        show language.es-the-black-cat-write k=2
      }
    }
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-the-black-cat-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
    try stars=3 {
      show language.es-the-black-cat-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  remember {
    say "The colour comes after the thing, and matches it: [[colour.the-black-cat]], [[colour.the-white-house]]."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is putting the colour first, as English does, and keeping the ending
    of a word for a man or boy after a word for a woman or girl. Every naming question offers
    exactly these two mistakes beside the right answer, so a wrong pick tells you which one the
    child made. This lesson turns on how the language puts a colour with a thing, so its questions
    are written for each language separately. The listening question is a dictation: read the
    sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, slowly and twice, or let the
    device play it. It is marked word by word, and a missing accent counts. If the child is stuck,
    write the two little words for "the" on two cards and put each colour under the card it goes
    with. Off the page, describe three toys with a colour each. It meets England's key stage 2
    languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "understand basic grammar appropriate
    to the language being studied, including (where relevant): feminine, masculine and neuter forms
    ... and how these differ from or are similar to English"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign
    languages: "To notice the differences between Japanese language and foreign languages about the
    sounds and letters, vocabulary, expressions, sentence structures"; and CEFR A1, "Can understand
    and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  language es {
    level easy {
      grown-ups "At this level la hierba verde is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are el gato negro and la casa blanca and the words to write are roja and amarilla, the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
    }
    level hard {
      grown-ups "At this level the words to name are el sol amarillo and la gata negra and the words to write are verde, blanco and roja, the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
    }
  }
}
